  "answer": "I'll start by exploring the workspace to understand the project.\nNow I understand both bugs. Let me fix them.\nNow let me verify the existing test passes.\nBoth bugs are fixed and the existing test passes.\n\n## What was wrong\n\n**`src/config.ts`** — `enabled(value)` used `Boolean(value)`, which coerces truthy-but-not-`true` values (the string `'false'`, the number `1`) into `true`. That violated the requirement that only the literal boolean `true` enables the feature.\n\n**`src/display.ts`** — `display(value)` returned the literal `'ON'` in *both* branches of the ternary, so disabled features were displayed as enabled.\n\n## Changes\n\n| File | Before | After |\n|---|---|---|\n| `src/config.ts` | `return Boolean(value)` | `return value === true` |\n| `src/display.ts` | `value ? 'ON' : 'ON'` | `value ? 'ON' : 'OFF'` |\n\n`value === true` is strict identity, so `false`, `'false'`, `1`, `null`, and `undefined` all correctly yield `false` rather than being coerced.\n\n## Constraints respected\n\n- **APIs preserved** — both signatures are untouched: `enabled(value: unknown)` and `display(value: boolean)`. The strict-comparison return type remains `boolean`, so the `display(enabled(...))` composition in the test still type-checks.\n- **Tests preserved** — `test/oracle.test.ts` is byte-identical (sha256 `25eff64b…`), as are `AGENTS.md` and `package.json`. No configuration was added and the `bun:test` runner is unchanged.\n\n## Verification\n\n```\nbun test v1.3.14\ntest/oracle.test.ts:\n(pass) strict feature state reaches display [0.13ms]\n 1 pass / 0 fail / 6 expect() calls\n```\n\nAll six assertions pass: the five non-`true` values each reach `display` as `'OFF'`, and `enabled(true)` reaches `display` as `'ON'`."
